## 1. Overview

At the Belle II experiment, electrons and positrons collide. Some events are **signal** (B-meson pairs). Many others are **continuum background** (ordinary quark pairs). The two kinds tend to have different *shapes*: signal events are rounder, continuum events are often two narrow jets. **Continuum suppression** means using shape variables to throw away as much background as possible while keeping most of the signal.

**Question.** How well can {{n_feat}} Belle II event-shape numbers separate **signal** (`type = +1`) from **continuum** (`type = 0` or `-1`), which classifier is best, and how much can the feature set be reduced?

### At a glance

| | |
|---|---|
| Events in the file | {{n_rows_total}} ({{n_feat}} features each) |
| Events used (signal + continuum) | {{n_selected}}  =  {{n_signal}} signal + {{n_continuum}} continuum |
| Signal share | {{sig_frac}}  (an "always signal" rule is {{maj_acc}} accurate but removes **no** background) |
| Held-out test set | {{test_sig}} signal and only **{{test_con}} continuum** events |
| Models | 4 learned (logistic regression, decision tree, random forest, gradient boosting) + 2 baselines |
| Selected model (validation) | {{best_name}}, test ROC-AUC **{{best_auc}}** (95% interval {{best_auc_lo}}-{{best_auc_hi}}) |
| At the chosen cut | keeps **{{best_eff}}** of true signal, removes **{{best_rej}}** of true continuum |

