## 5. Models and evaluation protocol

| Model | Idea in simple words | Features used |
|---|---|---|
| Majority class (baseline) | always answer "signal" | 0 |
| Single-feature cut (baseline) | one number, one cut (`{{single_feature}}`, picked from {{n_candidates}}) | 1 |
| Logistic regression | one straight dividing line through the feature space | {{n_feat}} |
| Decision tree | a chain of yes/no questions | {{n_feat}} |
| Random forest | many trees voting together | {{n_feat}} |
| Gradient boosting | trees added one by one, each fixing the last one's mistakes | {{n_feat}} |

- **Split:** stratified 70 / 15 / 15 (seed 42): {{train_sig}} / {{train_con}} train, {{val_sig}} / {{val_con}} validation, {{test_sig}} / {{test_con}} test (signal / continuum).
- **Class weighting:** compared fairly on validation data for every learned model (training labels only).
- **Operating point:** the score cut is chosen on the **validation** pile to remove as much continuum as possible while keeping at least {{floor}} of signal (the {{floor}} is our analysis choice, not an instructor requirement). It is frozen, then applied once to the test set.
- **Metrics:** signal efficiency (share of true signal kept), continuum rejection (share of true continuum removed), continuum acceptance (= 1 - rejection), accuracy, precision, recall, F1, balanced accuracy, macro-F1 and ROC-AUC (which does not depend on the cut).
- **Uncertainty:** exact (Clopper-Pearson) intervals for efficiency and rejection with their real denominators; stratified bootstrap for AUC; five different splits as an extra check.

